analysisWe need to parse the comment at end of previousSetBit method:

```java
// @@@ If fromIndex is greater than or equal to -1, then for every index i that is greater than the result and less than or equal to fromIndex, the bit at index i in the BitSet is false.
```

We need to produce an assert statement that matches this specification.

We have method previousSetBit(int fromIndex) returning int result (implicitly \result). The comment: If fromIndex >= -1, then for every index i that is greater than the result and <= fromIndex, the bit at index i is false.

So premise: fromIndex >= -1. Then conclusion: for all i, (result < i && i <= fromIndex) => !this.get(i). Actually "bit at index i in the BitSet is false" means get(i) == false.

Thus we need an assert: (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; result < i && i <= fromIndex; !this.get(i));

But need to refer to result variable \result. Use \result.

Thus:

assert (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; !this.get(i));

Make sure syntax: \forall int i; condition; spec. The condition is \result < i && i <= fromIndex. The spec is !this.get(i) (or this.get(i) == false). Use !this.get(i).

Thus final answer:

<code>assert (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; !this.get(i));</code>

Check if need parentheses around \result < i && i <= fromIndex, but fine.

Thus final.assistantfinal<code>assert (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; !this.get(i));</code>
